// What this is: the PRODUCTION wrangler configuration for the `identity`
// Worker.
//
// What this is NOT: it is not a working config on its own. Every resource id
// below is a PLACEHOLDER, and this file is safe to commit precisely because of
// that — see the secrets note before editing anything here.
//
// =================================================================================
// SECRETS (brief §2 constraint 27: "No production secrets committed")
//
// A Cloudflare resource id — a D1 `database_id`, a KV namespace `id`, a queue
// name — is injected at deploy time rather than committed. `secrets-management.md`
// is the owner of the classification and it calls these "Configuration, not a
// secret … public by construction", which is technically right: an id is a
// resource NAME, not a bearer credential on its own, and Cloudflare does not
// treat it as one.
//
// This repository is stricter anyway, and the strictness is a choice rather
// than a correction. An id is public in the sense that Cloudflare's own API
// will hand it to anyone who can authenticate, but it is not public in the
// sense that matters during an incident: publishing the production identity
// database's id tells an attacker exactly which resource to aim at, and
// removes one guess from the list. The cost of the strict position is one
// injection step per environment; the benefit is that a leaked clone, a fork, a
// CI log, or a screenshot of a diff names no production resource at all.
//
// If a future reader thinks this over-cautious: the classification table in
// `docs/security/secrets-management.md` is where that argument belongs, and
// changing it means changing that document and this one together. Do not
// resolve it by quietly committing the values here.
//
// The placeholders below are shaped to be obviously wrong, so a deploy that
// forgot to inject fails loudly with an unknown-resource error instead of
// silently pointing at something.
//
// `.release-please-manifest.json` names the three deployables and
// `.github/workflows/deploy-production.yml` injects the real ids into a
// generated copy of this file before wrangler runs. The generated copy is never
// committed and never uploaded as an artefact.
//
// `infra/cloudflare/vars.example` lists what an operator must set, and never
// the value.
// =================================================================================

{
  "$schema": "../../../../node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",

  "name": "identity",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-09-01",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],

  "main": "build/index.js",
  "build": {
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // THE COMMAND LOCATES THE REPOSITORY ROOT ITSELF. There is no `cwd` key
    // here, and both halves of that are deliberate.
    //
    // WHY NO `cwd`. Measured against wrangler 4.144.0, invoked from the
    // repository root with `-c infra/cloudflare/staging/identity/wrangler.jsonc`,
    // this is where a custom build actually runs for each spelling of `cwd`:
    //
    //   build.cwd      where the build ran
    //   ------------   ------------------------------------------------------
    //   (absent)       the INVOKING PROCESS's working directory
    //   "."            the invoking process's working directory — identical
    //   "../.."        the invoking process's working directory, plus ".."
    //   "$PWD"         Error: spawn /bin/sh ENOENT — `cwd` goes to spawn()
    //                  unexpanded, so a shell token makes wrangler try to
    //                  spawn the literal string as a directory
    //   "/abs/path"    exactly that path
    //
    // So `cwd` has no spelling that is both portable and caller-independent:
    // the relative ones are relative to whoever ran wrangler, and the absolute
    // one would hard-code `/home/<user>/…` into nine tracked configs that a
    // fork, a CI runner or a second contributor cannot use.
    //
    // WHY THE COMMAND THEN DOES THE LOCATING. `command -v wrangler` resolves
    // through node_modules to `<repo>/node_modules/.bin/wrangler`, so three
    // `dirname`s from it is the repository root — wherever the repository is,
    // whoever cloned it, and whichever directory wrangler was invoked from.
    //
    // THAT THIS WAS THE BUG. The command used to be
    //
    //   cd ../../../../apps/identity/worker && … --out-dir ../../../…
    //
    // with four `..` written for "the config's own directory". Every LOCAL
    // command and the `wrangler-validate` moon task do `cd` there first, so
    // four was right for them and the build worked on a laptop.
    // `deploy-worker.yml` does not: it runs wrangler from the REPOSITORY ROOT
    // against `-c infra/cloudflare/<env>/<worker>/wrangler.generated.jsonc`,
    // and four `..` from there leaves the repository entirely. The first
    // staging deploy failed with
    //
    //   [custom build] /bin/sh: 1: cd: can't cd to ../../../../apps/identity/worker
    //
    // — one level short, which is exactly what four levels up from the
    // repository root is. A custom build whose working directory depends on
    // who invoked wrangler is not a build; it is a build that happens to work
    // for the callers somebody happened to try first.
    //
    // `--out-dir` stays explicit because `worker-build` defaults it to `build`
    // relative to ITS OWN working directory — the crate — so the default writes
    // to `apps/<w>/worker/build/` while wrangler looks in
    // `infra/cloudflare/<env>/<w>/build/`, and reports "The expected output file
    // at build/index.js was not found", naming the path it could not find
    // rather than the one that was written.
    //
    // Verified from two different caller directories — the repository root and
    // the config's own — both of which land the build in `apps/<w>/worker` and
    // put the artefact where `main` points.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    "command": "ROOT=$(dirname \"$(dirname \"$(dirname \"$(command -v wrangler)\")\")\") && cd \"$ROOT/apps/identity/worker\" && cargo install -q \"worker-build@^0.8\" --locked && worker-build --release --out-dir \"$ROOT/infra/cloudflare/production/identity/build\"",
  },

  // Production logs are the record of what actually happened during an
  // incident. `persist: true` is what makes them queryable after the fact.
  // `redact_query_string: true` is not optional on an identity Worker: OAuth
  // authorization requests carry `code`, `state` and `nonce` in the query
  // string, and those are one-time credentials. Logging them unredacted turns
  // a log reader into a session thief.
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true,
    "redact_query_string": true,
    "logs": {
      "enabled": true,
      "head_sampling_rate": 1,
      "persist": true,
    },
    "traces": {
      "enabled": true,
      "head_sampling_rate": 0.1,
      "persist": true,
    },
    "issues": {
      "enabled": true,
    },
  },

  "d1_databases": [
    {
      "binding": "IDENTITY_DB",
      "database_name": "ecoma-identity",
      // PLACEHOLDER — injected at deploy time. brief §2 constraint 2: this
      // database is reachable from this Worker and from nowhere else in the
      // repository. There is no migration runner outside this Worker, no
      // admin-side connection string, and no script that opens it.
      "database_id": "PRODUCTION_IDENTITY_DB_DATABASE_ID",
      "migrations_dir": "../../../../database/identity/migrations",
      "migrations_table": "d1_migrations",
    },
  ],

  "kv_namespaces": [
    {
      "binding": "IDENTITY_KV",
      // PLACEHOLDER — injected at deploy time. brief §2 constraint 24: rate
      // limits and counters only, never authoritative identity state. A lost
      // namespace costs a day of accurate rate limiting and nothing else, which
      // is exactly why this state is in KV and not in D1.
      "id": "PRODUCTION_IDENTITY_KV_NAMESPACE_ID",
    },
  ],

  "queues": {
    "producers": [
      {
        "binding": "IDENTITY_QUEUE",
        // PLACEHOLDER — injected at deploy time. brief §2 constraint 22: the
        // envelope in contracts/events/v1/ is versioned so this producer stays
        // compatible with the consumer across a deploy of either side.
        "queue": "PRODUCTION_IDENTITY_QUEUE",
      },
    ],
  },

  "services": [
    {
      "binding": "EMAIL_PROVIDER",
      // PLACEHOLDER — injected at deploy time. brief §3 marks this binding
      // "deferred": the service exists as a binding and the code that calls it
      // is DEFERRED to the authentication-implementation phase. This file does
      // not claim the send path works.
      "service": "PRODUCTION_EMAIL_PROVIDER",
    },
  ],

  "ratelimits": [
    {
      "name": "RATE_LIMITER",
      // PLACEHOLDER — injected at deploy time. A dedicated production
      // namespace, not the staging one: a shared budget lets staging traffic
      // lock real users out of logging in.
      "namespace_id": "PRODUCTION_RATE_LIMITER_NAMESPACE_ID",
      "simple": {
        // 60/minute/IP on a login endpoint is deliberately conservative. Login
        // is the one place where "generous" and "a credential-stuffing
        // victim's account locked" are the same decision.
        "limit": 60,
        "period": 60,
      },
    },
  ],

  // Smart placement. `identity` makes a D1 round trip on nearly every request,
  // and D1 lives in a single region per database. Smart placement runs the
  // Worker next to that database instead of next to the user, which is the
  // right trade for a system where every request needs the database and few
  // requests are compute-bound. The cost is one extra network hop to the user;
  // on an identity endpoint the round trip to the session store dominates and
  // the hop does not.
  //
  // The consequence to know about: the Worker stops running in the colo nearest
  // the user, so `request.cf().colo` stops meaning "where the user is". Any
  // feature that later wants user geography must say so explicitly rather than
  // inferring it from colo.
  "placement": {
    "mode": "smart",
  },

  "vars": {
    // Non-secret configuration only. Secrets come from `wrangler secret put`
    // against the production Worker, never from this file and never from a
    // committed `.env` (brief §2 constraint 27).
    "ENVIRONMENT": "production",
    "ISSUER_BASE_URL": "https://identity.ecoma.io",
  },
}
